/**
\defgroup distrib_dirichlet Dirichlet (mean on the simplex, precision)
\brief Continuous distribution on the open simplex parametrized by mean \f$\mu\f$ in additive log-ratio coordinates and precision \f$\phi > 0\f$, with derivative blocks to order 6 and expected Fisher information to order 4.

Defined in `<laplace/distrib/dirichlet.hpp>` as \ref laplace::distrib::dirichlet_distrib "dirichlet_distrib", in namespace `laplace::distrib`. It follows the interface of \ref distrib_multivariate "Multivariate". Every member except `start_value()` is marked `LAPLACE_HD` so it runs on host CPUs and inside Kokkos kernels on the device. Across derivative blocks of orders \f$0\f$ to \f$6\f$, observation derivatives, and expected information to order \f$4\f$, it matches 60-digit `mpmath` references within \f$44.8\f$ `ulp1N` on `gcc` and \f$33.2\f$ `ulp1N` on CUDA.

## Classic form and parametrization

The classic Dirichlet distribution (Kotz, Balakrishnan and Johnson, 2000) on the open \f$d\f$-simplex (\f$d \ge 2\f$, \f$y_j > 0\f$ for \f$j = 1, \dots, d\f$, \f$\sum_{j=1}^d y_j = 1\f$) with shape parameters \f$\alpha_1, \dots, \alpha_d > 0\f$ and \f$\alpha_0 = \sum_{j=1}^d \alpha_j\f$ has density
\f[
f_{\mathrm{classic}}(y; \alpha) = \frac{\Gamma(\alpha_0)}{\prod_{j=1}^d \Gamma(\alpha_j)} \prod_{j=1}^d y_j^{\alpha_j - 1}.
\f]
Setting \f$\alpha_j = \phi \mu_j\f$ with precision \f$\phi > 0\f$ and mean \f$\mu\f$ on the open \f$d\f$-simplex (\f$\mu_j > 0\f$, \f$\sum_{j=1}^d \mu_j = 1\f$) gives \f$\alpha_0 = \phi\f$, \f$\mathbb{E}[Y] = \mu\f$, and \f$\operatorname{Cov}(Y) = (\operatorname{diag}\mu - \mu\mu^\top)/(\phi + 1)\f$. The implemented density and log-density are
\f[
f(y; \eta, \phi) = \frac{\Gamma(\phi)}{\prod_{j=1}^d \Gamma(\phi\mu_j)} \prod_{j=1}^d y_j^{\phi\mu_j - 1},
\qquad
\ell(\eta, \phi; y) = \log\Gamma(\phi) - \sum_{j=1}^d \log\Gamma(\alpha_j) + \sum_{j=1}^d (\alpha_j - 1)\log y_j.
\f]
With \f$d = 2\f$ categories, this reduces to \ref distrib_beta "the beta distribution" in mean and precision, with \f$\eta_1 = \operatorname{logit}(\mu_1)\f$.

\anchor distrib_dirichlet_coords
## Parameters and coordinates

Field `categories` holds \f$d \ge 2\f$, returned by `dim()`. The struct sets `normalization = distrib_normalization::proper`, `kind = distrib_kind::continuous`, and `n_params = 2`. In `params`, `mu` has kind `distrib_mv_param_kind::simplex_vector` (dense in `x`, `param_size(0)` equals \f$d - 1\f$) and `phi` has kind `distrib_mv_param_kind::scalar` on \f$(0, \infty)\f$ (indexed at \f$\theta\f$-index \f$0\f$, `theta_size()` equals \f$1\f$). Both parameters carry `regularity = infinitely_smooth`. `state_size()` is \f$0\f$, and `theta_state(theta, w)` returns \ref laplace::distrib::dirichlet_state "dirichlet_state" holding \f$\phi\f$ and \f$\log\phi\f$.

The mean \f$\mu\f$ is carried by its additive log-ratio coordinates \f$\eta_a = \log(\mu_a / \mu_d)\f$ for \f$a = 1, \dots, d - 1\f$ (Aitchison, 1986), with the last category as reference (\f$\eta_d = 0\f$):
\f[
\mu_j = \frac{e^{\eta_j}}{\sum_{k=1}^d e^{\eta_k}} \qquad (j = 1, \dots, d).
\f]
This is the map of `alr_bijector` (see \ref bijector_coupled "Coupled bijectors"). Here `dirichlet_distrib`, `probabilities()`, and `log_mu()` evaluate it directly by factoring out the largest category \f$c\f$ (\f$\eta_c = \max_k \eta_k\f$), computing \f$\log\mu_c = -\operatorname{log1p}\bigl(\sum_{k \ne c} e^{\eta_k - \eta_c}\bigr)\f$ and \f$\log\mu_j = (\eta_j - \eta_c) + \log\mu_c\f$ for \f$j \ne c\f$. A probability near \f$1\f$ therefore keeps its digits instead of cancelling against the log-sum-exp.

## Observation, support, and observation derivatives

The observation `y` stores all \f$d\f$ values as given; the last coordinate \f$y_d\f$ is not recomputed from the others. Member `in_support(y)` checks \f$y_j > 0\f$ for every \f$j = 1, \dots, d\f$ and \f$\lvert \sum_{j=1}^d y_j - 1 \rvert \le 10^{-8}\f$; elsewhere `logpdf()` returns \f$-\infty\f$.

Because \f$\sum_{j=1}^d y_j = 1\f$, derivatives in \f$y\f$ run over the \f$d - 1\f$ reference coordinates \f$y_1, \dots, y_{d-1}\f$ with \f$y_d = 1 - \sum_{a=1}^{d-1} y_a\f$, so `y_coord_size()` returns \f$d - 1\f$. At order \f$1\f$, for \f$a = 1, \dots, d - 1\f$,
\f[
\frac{\partial \ell}{\partial y_a} = \frac{\alpha_a - 1}{y_a} - \frac{\alpha_d - 1}{y_d}.
\f]
`logpdf_dy_dk<J, K, A...>(st, y, x, idx, out, w)` (\f$J + K + A \le 6\f$) forwards to `logpdf_dk<K, A...>()` when \f$J = 0\f$, and returns `false` without touching `out` when \f$J \ge 1\f$ and \f$K \ge 2\f$ (because \f$\alpha_j - 1\f$ is linear in \f$\phi\f$). For \f$J \ge 1\f$ and \f$K \in \{0, 1\}\f$, along a sorted \f$J\f$-tuple \f$s\f$ over \f$\{1, \dots, d - 1\}\f$ and a sorted \f$A\f$-tuple \f$t\f$ over \f$\eta\f$, it evaluates
\f[
[s = (a, \dots, a)]\, (-1)^{J-1} (J - 1)!\,\frac{\partial_t^K (\alpha_a - 1)}{y_a^J} - (J - 1)!\,\frac{\partial_t^K (\alpha_d - 1)}{y_d^J},
\f]
where \f$\partial_t^K (\alpha_j - 1)\f$ is \f$\phi\,\partial_t\mu_j\f$ for \f$K = 0, A \ge 1\f$, \f$\partial_t\mu_j\f$ for \f$K = 1, A \ge 1\f$, and \f$\mu_j\f$ for \f$K = 1, A = 0\f$. The output buffer `out` holds the order-\f$J\f$ tensor over \f$d - 1\f$ coordinates in Kronecker product (with \f$y\f$ first) with the order-\f$A\f$ tensor over \f$\eta\f$.

## Derivative blocks

`logpdf_dk<K, A...>(st, y, x, idx, out, w)` evaluates \f$K\f$ derivatives in \f$\phi\f$ (`idx` holds \f$K\f$ zeros) and \f$A\f$ derivatives in \f$\eta\f$ for \f$K + A \le 6\f$. `out` holds the packed symmetric tensor of order \f$A\f$ over the \f$d - 1\f$ coordinates of \f$\eta\f$ using the storage of \ref distrib_multivariate "Multivariate". For \f$K \ge 1\f$ and \f$A = 0\f$, the pure \f$\phi\f$ derivative is
\f[
\frac{\partial^K \ell}{\partial \phi^K} = \psi_{K-1}(\phi) - \sum_{j=1}^d \mu_j^K \psi_{K-1}(\alpha_j) + [K = 1]\sum_{j=1}^d \mu_j \log y_j.
\f]

\anchor distrib_dirichlet_accuracy_routes
## How the derivatives stay accurate

Three mechanisms prevent cancellation across extreme precisions and tiny shapes:

- **Per-coordinate split.** In \f$-\sum_{j=1}^d \log\Gamma(\alpha_j)\f$, categories with \f$\alpha_j < 1\f$ are rewritten as \f$-\log\Gamma(\alpha_j) = \log\alpha_j - \log\Gamma(1 + \alpha_j)\f$, where \f$\sum\log\alpha_j\f$ has closed derivatives through the cumulants and \f$-\log\Gamma(1 + \alpha_j)\f$ is differentiated via \ref faa_di_bruno "Faà di Bruno's formula" using \f$\psi_n(1 + \alpha_j)\f$. Categories with \f$\alpha_j \ge 1\f$ are rewritten as \f$-\log\Gamma(\alpha_j) = -(\alpha_j\log\alpha_j - \alpha_j) + \tfrac{1}{2}\log\alpha_j - \tfrac{1}{2}\log 2\pi - \delta(\alpha_j)\f$, where \f$\delta\f$ is Stirling's error (\ref stirling "stirling") differentiated through Faà di Bruno's formula. The leading terms have closed derivatives, and their parts linear in \f$\mu_j\f$ cancel identically across categories because \f$\sum_{j=1}^d \mu_j = 1\f$: they are summed over the small-\f$\alpha\f$ categories with reversed sign, and vanish identically when all \f$\alpha_j \ge 1\f$ or all \f$\alpha_j < 1\f$. Pure \f$\phi\f$ blocks evaluate the polygamma terms via \ref laplace::math::polygamma_monomial_remainder "polygamma_monomial_remainder()", cancelling the leading monomials algebraically. Without this split, blocks with two or more derivatives in \f$\phi\f$ lose up to \f$4.6 \times 10^{13}\f$ ulp at \f$\phi = 10^7\f$, and tiny shapes lose up to \f$3 \times 10^9\f$ ulp (measured with exact inputs; `docs/numerics.md`).
- **Double-double cumulant tables.** \anchor distrib_dirichlet_cumulants The derivatives of \f$\mu\f$ and \f$\log\mu\f$ in \f$\eta\f$ are the joint cumulants of the categorical indicators, evaluated by a recursion over the subsets of a tuple's positions in double-double arithmetic and rounded entry by entry. Each entry therefore stays accurate to its own magnitude even where it vanishes, such as where \f$1 - 2\mu = 0\f$. The unit's probabilities \f$\mu_j\f$ and complements \f$1 - \mu_j = \sum_{k \ne j}\mu_k\f$ are formed once per unit in double-double, and the tables live in `w` (`scratch_size()` doubles) rather than on the thread stack.
- **Data and Stirling combination.** Where \f$y\f$ is close to \f$\mu\f$ at large precision, the data term and Stirling's leading term on categories with \f$\alpha_j \ge 1\f$ are combined as \f$\log(y_j/\mu_j) = \operatorname{log1p}((y_j - \mu_j)/\mu_j)\f$ using double-double \f$\mu_j\f$. Likewise, pure \f$y\f$-derivatives in `logpdf_dy_dk<J, 0>()` are formed in double-double from the unit's probabilities to prevent cancellation between \f$(\alpha_a - 1)/y_a^J\f$ and \f$(\alpha_d - 1)/y_d^J\f$ near the mode.

## Expected Fisher information

Using \f$\mathbb{E}[\log Y_j] = \psi(\alpha_j) - \psi(\phi)\f$ and \f$\partial_a \mu_j = \mu_j(\delta_{ja} - \mu_a)\f$, the expected Fisher information blocks are
\f[
I_{\eta_a,\eta_b} = \phi^2 \sum_{j=1}^d \psi_1(\alpha_j)\,\partial_a\mu_j\,\partial_b\mu_j,
\qquad
I_{\eta_a,\phi} = \sum_{j=1}^d \partial_a\mu_j\,\alpha_j\!\left(\psi_1(\alpha_j) - \frac{1}{\alpha_j}\right),
\qquad
I_{\phi,\phi} = \sum_{j=1}^d \mu_j^2\,\psi_1(\alpha_j) - \psi_1(\phi).
\f]
`expected_info_dk<K, A...>(st, slot_a, slot_b, ab, x, idx, out, w)` differentiates these blocks \f$K\f$ times in \f$\phi\f$ and \f$A\f$ times in \f$\eta\f$ up to total order \f$K + A \le 4\f$, using the same per-coordinate split at \f$\alpha_j = 1\f$ and the double-double subset tables. Slot `(0, 0)` writes the packed symmetric matrix over \f$d - 1\f$ coordinates, `(0, theta_slot)` (or `(theta_slot, 0)`) writes a vector of length \f$d - 1\f$, and `(theta_slot, theta_slot)` writes one entry, each Kronecker-multiplied on the right by the order-\f$A\f$ tensor in \f$\eta\f$. Every call with \f$K + A \le 4\f$ returns `true`.

## Draws, moments, and starting values

`rng(st, x, seed, unit, y)` samples \f$\log G_j = \log G(\alpha_j + 1) + \log(U_j)/\alpha_j\f$, where \f$G(\alpha_j + 1)\f$ is a standard gamma variate from \ref laplace::distrib::standard_gamma_by_inversion "standard_gamma_by_inversion()" at draw \f$2(j - 1)\f$ and \f$U_j\f$ is uniform at draw \f$2(j - 1) + 1\f$ of \ref laplace::distrib::counter_uniform_source "counter_uniform_source" `{seed, unit}` (see \ref distrib_generic "Generic routes"), then sets \f$y_j = G_j / \sum_{k=1}^d G_k\f$ by log-sum-exp. This is exact in law and avoids \f$0/0\f$ at tiny shapes; a coordinate smaller than the least positive `double` rounds to \f$0\f$.

`mean(theta, x, out)` writes \f$\mu\f$, and `covariance(theta, x, out, w)` writes packed \f$(\operatorname{diag}\mu - \mu\mu^\top)/(\phi + 1)\f$ using \f$d\f$ doubles in `w`. On the host, `start_value(summary, theta, x)` computes \f$\eta_a = \log(\bar{y}_a / \bar{y}_d)\f$ from the sample mean \f$\bar{y}\f$ and sets \f$\phi = \max\bigl(\sum_{j=1}^d \bar{y}_j(1 - \bar{y}_j) / \sum_{j=1}^d \widehat{\operatorname{Var}}(Y_j) - 1,\, 10^{-3}\bigr)\f$ (defaulting to \f$10^6\f$ before flooring when the variance sum is not positive).

## Accuracy

See \ref accuracy "How accuracy is measured". References (`tools/reference/distrib_simplex.py`, `tests/data/reference/distrib_simplex.csv`; 11,262 values across both laws) were computed with `mpmath` (The mpmath development team, 2023) at 60 and 75 digits from the definition, over \f$d \in \{2, 3, 5\}\f$, \f$\phi \in [10^{-3}, 10^7]\f$, and shapes down to \f$10^{-12}\f$; the closed information forms agree with quadrature over the beta law for \f$d = 2\f$. Errors in `tests/distrib/test_simplex.cpp` are normwise `ulp1N` on `gcc` and CUDA:

| Quantity | Measured max (`gcc`) | Measured max (CUDA) | Test bound |
|---|---|---|---|
| Derivative blocks (\f$A + K \le 6\f$) | \f$44.8\f$ | \f$33.2\f$ | \f$68\f$ |
| Derivatives in \f$y\f$ | \f$14.3\f$ | \f$4.82\f$ | \f$22\f$ |
| Information \f$(\eta, \eta)\f$, orders 0–4 | \f$4.06\f$ | \f$4.27\f$ | \f$6.5\f$ |
| Information \f$(\eta, \phi)\f$, orders 0–4 | \f$4.83\f$ | \f$5.95\f$ | \f$9\f$ |
| Information \f$(\phi, \phi)\f$, orders 0–4 | \f$3.06\f$ | \f$11.1\f$ | \f$17\f$ |

Across \f$40{,}000\f$ draws (\f$d = 3\f$, \f$\phi = 8\f$), the sample mean stays within \f$4.5\f$ standard errors, the sample covariance stays within \f$4\%\f$ of the largest covariance entry, and coordinates sum to \f$1\f$ within \f$10^{-12}\f$; across \f$2{,}000\f$ draws at \f$\phi = 0.01\f$, every coordinate is finite and sums to \f$1\f$. Member `start_value()` recovers \f$\eta\f$ within \f$0.02\f$ and \f$\phi\f$ within \f$0.4\f$. See `docs/numerics.md`.

## Speed

Times from `benchmarks/distrib_simplex.cpp` over \f$20{,}000\f$ units (\f$\phi = 30\f$) in `docs/numerics.md`, in nanoseconds per unit:

| Dimension | Backend | Order 0 (ns) | Order 1 (ns) | Order 2 (ns) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| \f$3\f$ | OpenMP (16 threads) | \f$238\f$ | \f$297\f$ | \f$501\f$ |
| \f$3\f$ | CUDA | \f$66\f$ | \f$89\f$ | \f$185\f$ |
| \f$10\f$ | OpenMP (16 threads) | \f$888\f$ | \f$1229\f$ | \f$2616\f$ |
| \f$10\f$ | CUDA | \f$207\f$ | \f$313\f$ | \f$859\f$ |

## Example

Evaluating `logpdf()` and the \f$\eta\f$ gradient inside a Kokkos kernel:

\code{.cpp}
#include <Kokkos_Core.hpp>
#include <laplace/distrib/dirichlet.hpp>

void eval_dirichlet(const Kokkos::View<const double**>& y,
                    const Kokkos::View<const double**>& x,
                    double phi,
                    Kokkos::View<double*>& log_lik,
                    Kokkos::View<double**>& grad_eta) {
  constexpr int d = 3;
  const int n = static_cast<int>(y.extent(0));
  const laplace::distrib::dirichlet_distrib dist(d);
  Kokkos::View<double**, Kokkos::LayoutRight> work("work", n, dist.scratch_size());
  Kokkos::parallel_for(
      "dirichlet_eval", n, KOKKOS_LAMBDA(int i) {
        const double th[1] = {phi};
        double g[d - 1];
        const auto st = dist.theta_state(th, &work(i, 0));
        log_lik(i) = dist.logpdf(st, &y(i, 0), &x(i, 0));
        if (dist.template logpdf_dk<0, 1>(st, &y(i, 0), &x(i, 0), nullptr, g, &work(i, 0))) {
          for (int a = 0; a < d - 1; ++a) grad_eta(i, a) = g[a];
        }
      });
}
\endcode

## Module types

- \ref laplace::distrib::dirichlet_distrib "dirichlet_distrib" — Dirichlet distribution in mean \f$\mu\f$ (in additive log-ratio coordinates) and precision \f$\phi\f$
- \ref laplace::distrib::dirichlet_state "dirichlet_state" — precomputed state holding \f$\phi\f$ and \f$\log\phi\f$

## References

- J. Aitchison (1986), The Statistical Analysis of Compositional Data, Chapman and Hall. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-94-009-4109-0
- S. Kotz, N. Balakrishnan and N. L. Johnson (2000), Continuous Multivariate Distributions, vol. 1, 2nd ed., Wiley.
- The mpmath development team (2023), mpmath: a Python library for arbitrary-precision floating-point arithmetic, version 1.3.0, https://mpmath.org
*/

/**
\defgroup distrib_multinomial Multinomial (probabilities on the simplex)
\brief Discrete distribution on the integer simplex with known trial count and probabilities in additive log-ratio coordinates, with cumulant blocks to order 6 and expected information to order 4.

Defined in `<laplace/distrib/multinomial.hpp>` as \ref laplace::distrib::multinomial_distrib "multinomial_distrib", in namespace `laplace::distrib`. It follows the interface of \ref distrib_multivariate "Multivariate". Every member except `start_value()` is marked `LAPLACE_HD` so it runs on host CPUs and inside Kokkos kernels on the device. Across orders \f$0\f$ to \f$6\f$ and expected information to order \f$4\f$, it matches 60-digit `mpmath` references within \f$1.65\f$ `ulp1N` on both `gcc` and CUDA.

## Classic form and parametrization

The classic multinomial distribution (Johnson, Kotz and Balakrishnan, 1997) of \f$N \ge 1\f$ independent trials across \f$d \ge 2\f$ categories with category probabilities \f$p_1, \dots, p_d > 0\f$ (\f$\sum_{j=1}^d p_j = 1\f$) on counts \f$y_j \in \{0, 1, \dots, N\}\f$ summing to \f$N\f$ has probability mass
\f[
p_{\mathrm{classic}}(y; N, p) = \frac{N!}{\prod_{j=1}^d y_j!} \prod_{j=1}^d p_j^{y_j}.
\f]
Laplace carries the probabilities as `mu` (\f$\mu = p\f$) of kind `simplex_vector` in the additive log-ratio coordinates \f$\eta_1, \dots, \eta_{d-1}\f$ (\f$\eta_d = 0\f$; see \ref distrib_dirichlet_coords "Dirichlet coordinates") and stores \f$N\f$ as a trailing field `size` (an integer held in a `double`, as \ref distrib_binomial "binomial_distrib"'s `size`, excluded from `n_params` and `params`):
\f[
p(y; \eta) = \frac{N!}{\prod_{j=1}^d y_j!} \prod_{j=1}^d \mu_j^{y_j},
\qquad
\ell(\eta; y) = \log N! - \sum_{j=1}^d \log y_j! + \sum_{j=1}^d y_j \log\mu_j.
\f]
With \f$d = 2\f$ categories, this reduces to \ref distrib_binomial "the binomial distribution".

## Parameters and support

Field `categories` holds \f$d \ge 2\f$ (`dim()` returns \f$d\f$) and `size` holds \f$N \ge 1\f$. The struct sets `normalization = distrib_normalization::proper`, `kind = distrib_kind::discrete`, `n_params = 1` (`mu` of kind `simplex_vector`, `param_size(0)` equals \f$d - 1\f$, `regularity = infinitely_smooth`), `theta_size() = 0`, and `state_size() = 0`. `theta_state()` returns an empty \ref laplace::distrib::multinomial_state "multinomial_state". Because the law is discrete, there are no derivatives in \f$y\f$, and `y_coord_size()` returns \f$d\f$.

Member `in_support(y)` checks that every \f$y_j\f$ is a non-negative integer and \f$\sum_{j=1}^d y_j = N\f$; outside the support, `logpmf()` returns \f$-\infty\f$.

## Log-mass and derivative blocks

`logpmf(st, y, x)` evaluates the log-mass in the saddle-point form of \ref distrib_binomial "binomial_distrib" with Stirling's error \f$\delta\f$ (\ref stirling "stirling") and the unit deviance \f$D(1 + w) = (1 + w)\operatorname{log1p}(w) - w\f$, avoiding cancellation at large \f$N\f$:
\f[
\ell(\eta; y) = \tfrac{1}{2}\log(2\pi N) + \delta(N) - \sum_{y_j > 0}\!\left[\tfrac{1}{2}\log(2\pi y_j) + \delta(y_j) + N\mu_j\, D\!\left(1 + \frac{y_j - N\mu_j}{N\mu_j}\right)\right] - N\sum_{y_j = 0}\mu_j.
\f]
Because \f$\sum_{j=1}^d y_j \log\mu_j = \sum_{a=1}^{d-1} y_a \eta_a - N\log\!\bigl(1 + \sum_{a=1}^{d-1} e^{\eta_a}\bigr)\f$, `logpmf_dk<K, A...>(st, y, x, idx, out, w)` (\f$K + A \le 6\f$) returns `false` without touching `out` whenever \f$K > 0\f$ (no indexed parameters). For \f$K = 0\f$ and \f$A = 1\f$, it evaluates \f$y_a - N\mu_a\f$ in double-double from the unit's probabilities to prevent cancellation near the mode; for \f$K = 0\f$ and \f$2 \le A \le 6\f$, it writes \f$-N\kappa_t\f$, where \f$\kappa_t\f$ is the joint cumulant of the categorical indicators from \ref distrib_dirichlet_cumulants "the double-double subset tables".

## Expected information, draws, moments, and starting values

`expected_info_dk<K, A...>(st, slot_a, slot_b, ab, x, idx, out, w)` (\f$K + A \le 4\f$) returns `false` if \f$K > 0\f$ or either slot is not \f$0\f$. Otherwise, for `(0, 0)`, it writes \f$N\kappa_{ab}\f$ and its order-\f$A\f$ derivatives in \f$\eta\f$, \f$N\kappa_{abt}\f$ (the observed derivatives without \f$y\f$), laid out as the packed symmetric matrix over \f$d - 1\f$ coordinates in Kronecker product with the order-\f$A\f$ tensor in \f$\eta\f$.

`rng(st, x, seed, unit, y)` generates conditional binomials \f$Y_j \sim \operatorname{Bin}\!\bigl(N - \sum_{i < j} Y_i,\, \mu_j / \sum_{i \ge j} \mu_i\bigr)\f$ for \f$j = 1, \dots, d - 1\f$ by inversion via \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_quantile "distrib_quantile()" on `binomial_distrib` at draw \f$j - 1\f$ of \ref laplace::distrib::counter_uniform_source "counter_uniform_source" `{seed, unit}`, setting \f$Y_d\f$ to the remaining trials (for \f$d \le 64\f$). `mean(theta, x, out)` writes \f$N\mu\f$, `covariance(theta, x, out, w)` writes packed \f$N(\operatorname{diag}\mu - \mu\mu^\top)\f$ using \f$d\f$ doubles in `w`, and `start_value(summary, theta, x)` (host only) maps the pooled proportions \f$\bar{y} / N\f$ through the forward additive log-ratio map.

## Accuracy

See \ref accuracy "How accuracy is measured". Measured against the 60-digit `mpmath` references of `tools/reference/distrib_simplex.py` (`N` up to \f$10^6\f$) and test bounds of `tests/distrib/test_simplex.cpp`, errors in `ulp1N` are identical on `gcc` and CUDA:

| Quantity | Measured max (`gcc` and CUDA) | Test bound |
|---|---|---|
| Derivative blocks (\f$A \le 6\f$) | \f$1.65\f$ | \f$2.5\f$ |
| Expected information (\f$A \le 4\f$) | \f$0.717\f$ | \f$1.1\f$ |

Across \f$40{,}000\f$ draws (\f$d = 3\f$, \f$N = 20\f$), counts are non-negative integers summing to \f$N\f$ within \f$10^{-12}N\f$, the sample mean stays within \f$4.5\f$ standard errors, the sample covariance stays within \f$4\%\f$ of the largest covariance entry, and `start_value()` recovers \f$\eta\f$ within \f$0.02\f$. See `docs/numerics.md`.

## Speed

Times from `benchmarks/distrib_simplex.cpp` over \f$20{,}000\f$ units (\f$N = 50\f$) in `docs/numerics.md`, in nanoseconds per unit:

| Dimension | Backend | Order 0 (ns) | Order 1 (ns) | Order 2 (ns) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
